CLAIMS=[
('Spending problems are identity problems; you always act in alignment with your identity; identity acts like a thermostat.','it\'s actually an identity problem','Interpretive thesis drawing on identity-based habit ideas (popularised by James Clear\'s Atomic Habits, not cited). "Always" is an absolute claim without evidence. Opinion/framework, not a verifiable fact. '+U,[]),
('Acting outside your identity creates cognitive dissonance that pulls you back to old behaviour.','psychologists call cognitive dissonance','Cognitive dissonance (Festinger 1957) is an established concept, but the video applies it loosely. Dissonance theory predicts attitude change to fit behaviour as often as behaviour reverting to attitudes, which partly supports the "act first" advice. No study cited.',[]),
('Willpower is like a muscle that gets tired.','willpower is like a muscle that gets tired','The "ego depletion" model (Baumeister) failed large multi-lab replications (Hagger et al. 2016; Vohs et al. 2021), by analyst recall, not fetched. Presented as settled; CONTESTED.',[]),
('The first $1,000 is harder than the second; the first $10,000 is about building a different self.','the first thousand you save is so much harder','Motivational assertion; no data. '+U,[]),
('Saving $50 a month for a year is more powerful than $600 once, because of identity formation.','Saving $50 every month for a year is more powerful','Arithmetic: both total $600 (monthly saving actually earns slightly less interest than a lump sum at the start). The video concedes "not because of the math"; the identity effect is unsupported. '+U,[]),
('Lottery winners go broke within a few years because their identity never changed.','This is why lottery winners go broke','Popular but weak claim. Hankins, Hoekstra and Skiba (2011) found larger Florida winners delay rather than avoid bankruptcy. Swedish lottery studies (Cesarini et al.) find winners largely keep their wealth. Both by analyst recall, not fetched. OVERGENERALISED.',[]),
('Having $10,000 creates "walking away money", confidence that leads to raises and promotions.','walking away money','Plausible mechanism (outside options improve bargaining), but the raise/promotion feedback loop is asserted from anecdote ("I\'ve seen this happen"). '+U,[]),
('The first $10,000 rewrites your brain chemistry; living paycheck to paycheck floods the brain with cortisol; a buffer lowers it ("actual neuroscience").','The first $10,000 rewrites your brain chemistry','Partly grounded: financial scarcity is linked to stress and reduced cognitive bandwidth (Mani et al. 2013), and cash transfers lowered cortisol in a Kenyan RCT (Haushofer & Shapiro), by analyst recall. The specific $10,000 threshold and "rewrites brain chemistry" are UNSUPPORTED overstatements.',[]),
('People who understand money best intellectually often struggle most to save.','the people who struggle the most with money are often the ones who understand it the best','Anecdotal; financial-literacy research generally finds positive (if modest) associations between knowledge and saving. UNSUPPORTED as stated. '+U,[]),
('Narrator spent 3 years reading about investing with money in savings, then invested $50 in an index fund.','I spent 3 years reading about investing','Personal anecdote; not verifiable.',[]),
('A self-made person would rebuild $100,000 faster than an inheritor; the first $10,000 is more valuable than the second $100,000.','your first $10,000 is so much more valuable than your second 100,000','Rhetorical thought experiment; no evidence. '+U,[]),
('After about six months of consistent behaviour, saving starts to feel automatic.','after six months of consistently acting','Habit-formation research (Lally et al. 2010) found a median of about 66 days with wide variation (18-254), by analyst recall. A six-month figure is within range, but presented without a source. '+U,[]),
]
